WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.308 --> 00:00:03.333
- He's an entrepreneur,
scientist, and author.

00:00:03.333 --> 00:00:06.426
He's the co-founder and
chairman of BodyMedia.

00:00:06.426 --> 00:00:10.252
Since 2010, he's been directing
Google X Laboratories.

00:00:10.252 --> 00:00:11.531
He's Astro Teller,

00:00:11.531 --> 00:00:14.055
and here are his top 10 rules for success.

00:00:14.055 --> 00:00:17.438
(dramatic swoosh)

00:00:17.438 --> 00:00:19.105
- Go big or go home.

00:00:20.726 --> 00:00:24.986
It's so easy to think
that if you play it safe,

00:00:24.986 --> 00:00:28.568
if you do it a little
simpler, a little smaller,

00:00:28.568 --> 00:00:30.321
you won't scare yourself,

00:00:30.321 --> 00:00:32.827
you won't scare your friends, your family,

00:00:32.827 --> 00:00:34.936
you won't scare your investors

00:00:34.936 --> 00:00:36.901
when you go ask them for money.

00:00:36.901 --> 00:00:38.745
It's just not true.

00:00:38.745 --> 00:00:40.712
The world just doesn't work like that.

00:00:40.712 --> 00:00:42.142
It's counterintuitive,

00:00:42.142 --> 00:00:45.819
but you can't go too
big, you really can't.

00:00:45.819 --> 00:00:50.167
The bigger you go the more
inspired you will be personally.

00:00:50.167 --> 00:00:52.405
And trust me, you will need to be inspired

00:00:52.405 --> 00:00:56.284
to get through the hard
times that will hit you.

00:00:56.284 --> 00:01:00.084
The bigger you go the more
of a cause you can make it

00:01:00.084 --> 00:01:03.453
for the people who you're
going to need to attract.

00:01:03.453 --> 00:01:04.559
"Hey, come with me.

00:01:04.559 --> 00:01:07.975
"We're going to do something
and make the world 5% better."

00:01:07.975 --> 00:01:11.640
Eh, you know what, I'll
stay in my current job.

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:13.681
We're going to make the
world 10 times better.

00:01:13.681 --> 00:01:16.310
We're going to make the
world 100 times better.

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:17.192
Now you've got me.

00:01:17.192 --> 00:01:19.191
Now it's like I'll ask
what I'm getting paid

00:01:19.191 --> 00:01:21.151
three months after I start work, right?

00:01:21.151 --> 00:01:23.275
That's the feeling that you need

00:01:23.275 --> 00:01:25.055
to be able to create in people

00:01:25.055 --> 00:01:28.260
in order for them to join you.

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:30.635
So go big or go home.

00:01:30.635 --> 00:01:33.385
The second thing is failing fast.

00:01:34.223 --> 00:01:39.047
Companies say that phrase,
but they just don't do it.

00:01:39.047 --> 00:01:41.719
In order to do something radical,

00:01:41.719 --> 00:01:43.896
you have to be prepared
for most of the things,

00:01:43.896 --> 00:01:47.533
literally 99% of the things
that you do not to work out,

00:01:47.533 --> 00:01:50.505
which is totally fine and
it's not too expensive

00:01:50.505 --> 00:01:53.303
if you kill them at the first moment

00:01:53.303 --> 00:01:57.738
when you could reasonably
have known to stop doing it.

00:01:57.738 --> 00:02:02.183
But for all kinds of mostly
emotional reasons we don't.

00:02:02.183 --> 00:02:03.880
I mean even we don't at Google X.

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:07.214
It's incredibly hard to make this happen.

00:02:07.214 --> 00:02:10.633
If you're going to have a portfolio
of these more radical bets,

00:02:10.633 --> 00:02:12.383
of trying things out,

00:02:13.263 --> 00:02:16.107
I'm not and I assure you you're not

00:02:16.107 --> 00:02:18.372
smart enough to figure out ahead of time

00:02:18.372 --> 00:02:20.566
which of the hundred
things you're going to try

00:02:20.566 --> 00:02:21.824
is the one that's going to work out.

00:02:21.824 --> 00:02:23.144
There's just no way.

00:02:23.144 --> 00:02:25.100
Like maybe one person in a generation,

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:27.889
like a Steve Jobs like person
can just point and say,

00:02:27.889 --> 00:02:29.666
"That turns out to be the right answer."

00:02:29.666 --> 00:02:32.886
So unless you're the Steve
Jobs of this generation,

00:02:32.886 --> 00:02:36.201
the right answer is try
stuff, just try stuff.

00:02:36.201 --> 00:02:39.597
But then you have to create
a culture among your people

00:02:39.597 --> 00:02:43.548
that says, "I value you trying things.

00:02:43.548 --> 00:02:47.230
"There's nothing wrong
with the learning moment."

00:02:47.230 --> 00:02:49.565
The failure moment is not the moment

00:02:49.565 --> 00:02:51.845
where we stop a project.

00:02:51.845 --> 00:02:54.523
It is the moment when we
decide to stop a project

00:02:54.523 --> 00:02:57.768
and realize that we
knew six months earlier,

00:02:57.768 --> 00:03:01.002
a year and a half earlier,
that we could have killed it.

00:03:01.002 --> 00:03:02.373
That's the failure moment,

00:03:02.373 --> 00:03:05.381
not the moment six months
earlier or 18 months earlier

00:03:05.381 --> 00:03:07.079
where we say, "Oh, that was worth trying.

00:03:07.079 --> 00:03:09.043
"Didn't work out, next."

00:03:09.043 --> 00:03:09.890
That's a learning moment.

00:03:09.890 --> 00:03:11.609
That's not a failure moment.

00:03:11.609 --> 00:03:14.510
The perspective shift
is what it's all about.

00:03:14.510 --> 00:03:18.127
That why going 10 times
bigger is more important

00:03:18.127 --> 00:03:22.184
and works better than going 10% bigger.

00:03:22.184 --> 00:03:25.326
When you shoot for 10% better
you're in a smartness contest.

00:03:25.326 --> 00:03:27.157
You're putting all of your people

00:03:27.157 --> 00:03:30.076
in a smartness contest with
everyone else in the world.

00:03:30.076 --> 00:03:31.558
They're not going to win.

00:03:31.558 --> 00:03:33.844
It doesn't matter how
much money you give them.

00:03:33.844 --> 00:03:35.046
But, if you push them,

00:03:35.046 --> 00:03:36.671
if you give them the expectation

00:03:36.671 --> 00:03:39.802
and the freedom to be weird,

00:03:39.802 --> 00:03:41.773
that's moonshot thinking.

00:03:41.773 --> 00:03:44.374
Then they can do the perspective shifts.

00:03:44.374 --> 00:03:47.264
And not only if you
shoot for 10 times bigger

00:03:47.264 --> 00:03:49.008
instead of 10% bigger,

00:03:49.008 --> 00:03:51.116
it's almost never 100 times harder

00:03:51.116 --> 00:03:52.595
and the payoff is 100 times,

00:03:52.595 --> 00:03:53.763
so you already know that you've got

00:03:53.763 --> 00:03:55.261
a better return on your investment.

00:03:55.261 --> 00:03:57.658
But sometimes it's literally easier,

00:03:57.658 --> 00:04:00.539
and the reason is because
that perspective shift

00:04:00.539 --> 00:04:01.746
is actually cheap

00:04:01.746 --> 00:04:04.596
relatively to being smarter
than everyone around you.

00:04:04.596 --> 00:04:06.237
No excuses.

00:04:06.237 --> 00:04:07.904
No victim mentality.

00:04:08.874 --> 00:04:11.819
The world owes you nothing.

00:04:11.819 --> 00:04:16.229
The world might come help you,
but I wouldn't count on it.

00:04:16.229 --> 00:04:18.312
And if you start from the

00:04:19.266 --> 00:04:22.998
feeling that you need
the world to participate

00:04:22.998 --> 00:04:24.712
in order for you to be successful

00:04:24.712 --> 00:04:28.102
at taking on this new thing
that you're considering doing,

00:04:28.102 --> 00:04:29.716
you're right to be scared.

00:04:29.716 --> 00:04:30.705
It might.

00:04:30.705 --> 00:04:32.657
I wouldn't count on it though.

00:04:32.657 --> 00:04:33.984
But if you start from the perspective

00:04:33.984 --> 00:04:37.837
that the world owes you
nothing, which it doesn't,

00:04:37.837 --> 00:04:42.141
and that it is not likely to
bring you any particular help,

00:04:42.141 --> 00:04:44.111
you have nothing to lose,

00:04:44.111 --> 00:04:47.429
because really isn't that
for every one of the people

00:04:47.429 --> 00:04:48.513
you brought onto the team,

00:04:48.513 --> 00:04:50.487
isn't that how you want them to feel

00:04:50.487 --> 00:04:52.097
when they report to you?

00:04:52.097 --> 00:04:53.171
You don't want excuses.

00:04:53.171 --> 00:04:54.740
That doesn't mean that you can't fail

00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:56.486
or that they can't ever fail.

00:04:56.486 --> 00:04:59.960
But right when you're starting,
you have nothing to lose.

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:02.210
When you're a huge company,

00:05:03.258 --> 00:05:06.566
you can spend 99% of your time

00:05:06.566 --> 00:05:08.896
worrying how not to mess up

00:05:08.896 --> 00:05:12.788
the billion dollar a year
money hose you've created.

00:05:12.788 --> 00:05:14.339
But, you don't have a
billion dollar a year

00:05:14.339 --> 00:05:15.346
money hose right now.

00:05:15.346 --> 00:05:17.701
You have nothing, so you can't lose.

00:05:17.701 --> 00:05:19.642
Spend 99% of your time

00:05:19.642 --> 00:05:22.298
imagining what would
happen if things went well

00:05:22.298 --> 00:05:23.688
and planning for that.

00:05:23.688 --> 00:05:25.950
I think risk is important
for every company.

00:05:25.950 --> 00:05:28.745
I think it is the essence
of entrepreneurship

00:05:28.745 --> 00:05:30.773
whether you're the smallest
company in the world

00:05:30.773 --> 00:05:33.175
or like Google the second
largest company in the world

00:05:33.175 --> 00:05:35.675
to take smart risks over time.

00:05:36.884 --> 00:05:39.730
I run this experiment
frequently with groups

00:05:39.730 --> 00:05:42.963
where I give them two choices,
Choice A and Choice B.

00:05:42.963 --> 00:05:44.755
So Choice A is you get to deliver

00:05:44.755 --> 00:05:47.275
a million dollars of bottom line value

00:05:47.275 --> 00:05:50.314
to your company this year, guaranteed.

00:05:50.314 --> 00:05:52.500
Choice B you get to deliver

00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:54.991
a billion dollars of bottom line value

00:05:54.991 --> 00:05:55.953
to your company this year,

00:05:55.953 --> 00:05:57.974
but only with one chance in 100.

00:05:57.974 --> 00:05:59.555
So everyone does the mental math.

00:05:59.555 --> 00:06:00.863
I say, "Who wants Choice A?"

00:06:00.863 --> 00:06:02.237
Nobody raises their hand.

00:06:02.237 --> 00:06:03.799
"Who wants Choice B?"

00:06:03.799 --> 00:06:04.779
Everybody raises their hand.

00:06:04.779 --> 00:06:06.052
It's got an expected utility

00:06:06.052 --> 00:06:08.325
that's 10 times higher than Choice A.

00:06:08.325 --> 00:06:10.732
And then I say, "Who here
believes that your boss

00:06:10.732 --> 00:06:13.049
"wants you to choose Choice B?"

00:06:13.049 --> 00:06:15.600
And not a single person in
the room raises their hand.

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:18.180
I mean these typically
are large multinationals

00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:20.532
who want to be innovative.

00:06:20.532 --> 00:06:23.417
And then I say, "You don't
need a lecture on innovation.

00:06:23.417 --> 00:06:24.718
"You need a new boss.

00:06:24.718 --> 00:06:26.383
"You need a new context."

00:06:26.383 --> 00:06:28.505
I really think that it's
the context that matters.

00:06:28.505 --> 00:06:32.374
We all inherently understand
that taking smart risks

00:06:32.374 --> 00:06:33.860
is the right thing to do.

00:06:33.860 --> 00:06:36.238
Now what counts as smart
and what counts as a risk

00:06:36.238 --> 00:06:38.654
is different in different
parts of the company

00:06:38.654 --> 00:06:41.094
or companies of different sizes.

00:06:41.094 --> 00:06:45.495
You know, you can tolerate
a PR fiasco much easier

00:06:45.495 --> 00:06:48.211
if you're a tiny company,
because no one is watching you,

00:06:48.211 --> 00:06:50.075
than if you're a large
company for example.

00:06:50.075 --> 00:06:51.519
So, the calculus is different,

00:06:51.519 --> 00:06:53.359
but the principle is still the same.

00:06:53.359 --> 00:06:54.959
The only way to stay sharp,

00:06:54.959 --> 00:06:57.213
to stay growing, to stay relevant,

00:06:57.213 --> 00:06:59.742
is to take smart risks all the time.

00:06:59.742 --> 00:07:00.840
This is actually what I spend

00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:03.444
a lot of my time on with Google X

00:07:03.444 --> 00:07:07.985
that if we all believe that
our project has to work,

00:07:07.985 --> 00:07:09.514
then we're going to find some way

00:07:09.514 --> 00:07:12.176
to BS ourselves, to BS our manager

00:07:12.176 --> 00:07:13.245
that it's going to work

00:07:13.245 --> 00:07:15.538
even way past the point
where we know in our hearts

00:07:15.538 --> 00:07:16.496
that it's not going to work.

00:07:16.496 --> 00:07:17.811
And we do that because we're worried

00:07:17.811 --> 00:07:19.504
we're going to lose our bonuses,

00:07:19.504 --> 00:07:21.362
we're going to lose the
people who report to us,

00:07:21.362 --> 00:07:22.641
we might lose our jobs,

00:07:22.641 --> 00:07:25.178
we're going to lose face with our peers.

00:07:25.178 --> 00:07:26.341
We have all these reasons

00:07:26.341 --> 00:07:28.129
not to want to raise our hands and say,

00:07:28.129 --> 00:07:29.227
"Look, this is a dumb one.

00:07:29.227 --> 00:07:30.795
"Let's go do something else."

00:07:30.795 --> 00:07:33.015
And so if you can create a culture

00:07:33.015 --> 00:07:34.930
in which people actually believe

00:07:34.930 --> 00:07:37.996
not that failure in the
long run is acceptable like,

00:07:37.996 --> 00:07:40.350
"Oh, we just kind of went in
circles, but that's cool."

00:07:40.350 --> 00:07:41.961
That's not cool.

00:07:41.961 --> 00:07:44.094
But, if you can see each thing that you do

00:07:44.094 --> 00:07:47.861
as a purposeful experiment,
there's no bad ...

00:07:47.861 --> 00:07:49.045
There are bad experiments to run.

00:07:49.045 --> 00:07:51.015
Like if you already ran the
experiment you just run it again

00:07:51.015 --> 00:07:52.572
that's a dumb thing to do,

00:07:52.572 --> 00:07:54.502
but there's no bad
outcome to an experiment

00:07:54.502 --> 00:07:56.742
if it was a thoughtful experiment to run.

00:07:56.742 --> 00:07:58.183
Think of a good question.

00:07:58.183 --> 00:07:59.663
Think of the fastest, cheapest way

00:07:59.663 --> 00:08:01.358
you can answer that question.

00:08:01.358 --> 00:08:03.514
Make sure that the answer to the question

00:08:03.514 --> 00:08:04.973
would actually change what it is

00:08:04.973 --> 00:08:06.495
that we would do afterwards.

00:08:06.495 --> 00:08:07.810
Go run the experiment.

00:08:07.810 --> 00:08:08.881
And then you can celebrate

00:08:08.881 --> 00:08:10.886
whatever the answer is afterwards.

00:08:10.886 --> 00:08:13.418
And that way of making progress,

00:08:13.418 --> 00:08:16.224
I think that's the rate limiting
step of real innovation.

00:08:16.224 --> 00:08:17.971
Swim out of your element.

00:08:17.971 --> 00:08:20.728
That's another one my favorite ones.

00:08:20.728 --> 00:08:22.978
I chronically take on tasks

00:08:24.022 --> 00:08:26.494
that I'm hopelessly unqualified to do,

00:08:26.494 --> 00:08:28.354
and I do it on purpose.

00:08:28.354 --> 00:08:30.936
So, I know that feeling
that you have inside

00:08:30.936 --> 00:08:32.418
where you're thinking,

00:08:32.418 --> 00:08:34.532
"Nano tech, and I know what to do,"

00:08:34.532 --> 00:08:36.766
and then you think, "But
I don't actually know

00:08:36.766 --> 00:08:38.419
"anything about nano tech.

00:08:38.419 --> 00:08:41.405
"I need my head examined
for even considering

00:08:41.405 --> 00:08:44.166
"going and changing an
industry I know nothing about."

00:08:44.166 --> 00:08:45.062
Right?

00:08:45.062 --> 00:08:46.187
Wrong.

00:08:46.187 --> 00:08:48.245
Swim out of your element.

00:08:48.245 --> 00:08:51.743
First of all, a lot of great ideas ...

00:08:51.743 --> 00:08:54.485
You're not going to be
smarter than everybody else,

00:08:54.485 --> 00:08:56.455
so the fact that you're not
in nano tech doesn't matter

00:08:56.455 --> 00:08:57.647
because if you were in nano tech

00:08:57.647 --> 00:08:59.836
you still wouldn't be in the top 10 people

00:08:59.836 --> 00:09:01.538
in the field of nano tech.

00:09:01.538 --> 00:09:05.705
So the fact that you aren't
the top 10 is irrelevant.

00:09:06.552 --> 00:09:08.954
You aren't going to work
harder than everybody else.

00:09:08.954 --> 00:09:09.947
You're probably not going to have

00:09:09.947 --> 00:09:11.597
more funding than everybody else.

00:09:11.597 --> 00:09:14.618
Those are not ways you
could have won anyway.

00:09:14.618 --> 00:09:16.073
You can be more bold.

00:09:16.073 --> 00:09:18.863
You can be more creative.

00:09:18.863 --> 00:09:20.182
Those things you can actually do.

00:09:20.182 --> 00:09:22.493
But you don't need to
be from their industry

00:09:22.493 --> 00:09:25.257
in order to have those qualities.

00:09:25.257 --> 00:09:29.780
And you can transplant
your ideas, your expertise

00:09:29.780 --> 00:09:34.181
from an area that you are an
expert in into a new area.

00:09:34.181 --> 00:09:36.914
If you're already in an industry

00:09:36.914 --> 00:09:38.374
you've already been brainwashed

00:09:38.374 --> 00:09:42.171
with the rest of the industry
into how that industry works.

00:09:42.171 --> 00:09:44.283
Your chances of breaking that industry

00:09:44.283 --> 00:09:46.229
and remaking it in a productive way

00:09:46.229 --> 00:09:49.694
are actually meaningfully lower.

00:09:49.694 --> 00:09:51.694
If you take ideas, data,

00:09:52.939 --> 00:09:56.355
inspirations with you into a new industry

00:09:56.355 --> 00:09:59.943
you can actually have a
perspective that they haven't had,

00:09:59.943 --> 00:10:00.776
and that you can do.

00:10:00.776 --> 00:10:02.102
Even if you can't be smarter,

00:10:02.102 --> 00:10:04.266
or better funded, or work more hours,

00:10:04.266 --> 00:10:06.450
you can have a perspective
that they don't have

00:10:06.450 --> 00:10:09.814
because of your naivety in that industry.

00:10:09.814 --> 00:10:11.381
So, swimming out of your element

00:10:11.381 --> 00:10:13.724
is not a reason not to start.

00:10:13.724 --> 00:10:16.480
It's not a handicap that you have.

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:17.975
You can't use it as an excuse.

00:10:17.975 --> 00:10:19.851
It's actually your secret weapon.

00:10:19.851 --> 00:10:21.929
You should look at that as an opportunity

00:10:21.929 --> 00:10:24.495
to start in a new area.

00:10:24.495 --> 00:10:25.690
Plus personally,

00:10:25.690 --> 00:10:28.590
in any fight I'd rather be the
underdog than the champion.

00:10:28.590 --> 00:10:30.460
It's like remember in high school

00:10:30.460 --> 00:10:32.577
when you were like playing
against the varsity team

00:10:32.577 --> 00:10:33.862
and you were junior varsity?

00:10:33.862 --> 00:10:36.975
That felt awesome, because
you couldn't lose, right?

00:10:36.975 --> 00:10:39.169
If you lost, you were supposed to lose.

00:10:39.169 --> 00:10:43.217
And if you win, then you
get bragging rights forever.

00:10:43.217 --> 00:10:44.050
It's the same thing.

00:10:44.050 --> 00:10:47.392
When you enter a new industry,
you have nothing to lose.

00:10:47.392 --> 00:10:50.289
Let's not build a prototype

00:10:50.289 --> 00:10:52.037
and do all the easy stuff first

00:10:52.037 --> 00:10:55.167
so that it looks like we're
making progress every week.

00:10:55.167 --> 00:10:56.288
I don't want that,

00:10:56.288 --> 00:10:59.148
because we're in danger of
building a ladder to the Moon

00:10:59.148 --> 00:11:00.727
where we're closer to the Moon every week

00:11:00.727 --> 00:11:03.076
but we're never really going to get there.

00:11:03.076 --> 00:11:05.510
Let's work on the hardest part first.

00:11:05.510 --> 00:11:08.971
We discuss for the project
that you're going to work on

00:11:08.971 --> 00:11:10.858
what are the things that are most likely

00:11:10.858 --> 00:11:12.094
to kill the project?

00:11:12.094 --> 00:11:15.507
And I say, "Your job is not to
cause the project to succeed.

00:11:15.507 --> 00:11:18.335
"Your job is to kill your project.

00:11:18.335 --> 00:11:20.081
"I want you to kill your project.

00:11:20.081 --> 00:11:21.205
"You won't be fired.

00:11:21.205 --> 00:11:22.888
"We're just going to keep doing this."

00:11:22.888 --> 00:11:27.429
And it is only the process of
failing to kill your project

00:11:27.429 --> 00:11:30.058
that will eventually cause it to succeed.

00:11:30.058 --> 00:11:31.776
So I tell you this.

00:11:31.776 --> 00:11:34.537
You're thinking, "Does
he really mean that?

00:11:34.537 --> 00:11:36.054
"That sounds scary."

00:11:36.054 --> 00:11:39.013
When you do that the first couple of times

00:11:39.013 --> 00:11:40.752
and you try an experiment,

00:11:40.752 --> 00:11:43.159
which is functionally
what these things are,

00:11:43.159 --> 00:11:46.084
maybe it works and we take
that risk off the list.

00:11:46.084 --> 00:11:46.917
That's awesome.

00:11:46.917 --> 00:11:48.250
We de-risked your project.

00:11:48.250 --> 00:11:50.855
We try a few more and
we find the fatal flaw.

00:11:50.855 --> 00:11:53.707
Yes indeed, this was going
to be a ladder to the Moon.

00:11:53.707 --> 00:11:56.647
And so at Google X the mantra,

00:11:56.647 --> 00:11:59.370
I know how Pollyannaish this sounds,

00:11:59.370 --> 00:12:02.683
but we want to make the world
a radically better place.

00:12:02.683 --> 00:12:04.731
And we have conversations all the time.

00:12:04.731 --> 00:12:07.792
At the end of the conversation we say,

00:12:07.792 --> 00:12:09.927
"Nope, that would make a
lot of money for Google.

00:12:09.927 --> 00:12:11.532
"Wouldn't make the world
a radically better place.

00:12:11.532 --> 00:12:12.505
"Next."

00:12:12.505 --> 00:12:13.838
And it's joyous.

00:12:15.792 --> 00:12:17.567
And I believe that it's actually

00:12:17.567 --> 00:12:21.633
financially smart for Google
to have us think like that,

00:12:21.633 --> 00:12:23.800
because then they get a bunch of people

00:12:23.800 --> 00:12:27.353
who all are as excited as I am
to run to work in the morning

00:12:27.353 --> 00:12:29.137
because I get to be in a place

00:12:29.137 --> 00:12:31.111
where we get to say that to each other.

00:12:31.111 --> 00:12:35.513
It's just so invigorating
that you get to say that

00:12:35.513 --> 00:12:37.195
and then everyone else
starts saying it, too.

00:12:37.195 --> 00:12:38.028
They're like, "Really?"

00:12:38.028 --> 00:12:40.903
Because don't you all want
to be able to say that

00:12:40.903 --> 00:12:42.136
in your daily lives

00:12:42.136 --> 00:12:44.061
that you're not just trying to make money

00:12:44.061 --> 00:12:45.321
for yourself or someone else,

00:12:45.321 --> 00:12:46.956
that you want to make the world better.

00:12:46.956 --> 00:12:49.041
It's just fundamentally inspirational.

00:12:49.041 --> 00:12:51.844
I think it's a basic part of being a human

00:12:51.844 --> 00:12:55.602
that we crave that like we
crave love or community.

00:12:55.602 --> 00:12:58.875
And if you say, "That's what
we're going to be like ..."

00:12:58.875 --> 00:13:01.776
You know, Google X is
another one of these,

00:13:01.776 --> 00:13:05.943
this is a separate but
related Santa Clause moment.

00:13:07.183 --> 00:13:09.950
At Google X we don't worry,

00:13:09.950 --> 00:13:13.467
we don't work towards is probably
a better way of saying it,

00:13:13.467 --> 00:13:15.144
how we're going to make money.

00:13:15.144 --> 00:13:17.591
In the early conversations
about our moonshots

00:13:17.591 --> 00:13:19.303
they do not include,

00:13:19.303 --> 00:13:21.513
"Okay, so then it would cost us this much.

00:13:21.513 --> 00:13:23.030
"And then we could charge that much.

00:13:23.030 --> 00:13:25.532
"And that nets out positive."

00:13:25.532 --> 00:13:28.334
Nope, it's the wrong
way to think about it.

00:13:28.334 --> 00:13:31.167
Because if you're going to
make the world slightly better

00:13:31.167 --> 00:13:33.063
then you have to claw for those pennies

00:13:33.063 --> 00:13:35.560
that you're going to make.

00:13:35.560 --> 00:13:37.207
That's a tough business.

00:13:37.207 --> 00:13:39.136
It's just exhausting.

00:13:39.136 --> 00:13:42.353
If you make the world seriously better,

00:13:42.353 --> 00:13:45.186
10x better than the alternative,

00:13:45.186 --> 00:13:46.874
the money will come find you.

00:13:46.874 --> 00:13:48.705
You really don't have to figure it out.

00:13:48.705 --> 00:13:51.364
I can see from the outside
how frustrating this looks

00:13:51.364 --> 00:13:53.568
to watch Google do this
over and over again.

00:13:53.568 --> 00:13:54.898
Because I think there's this attitude like

00:13:54.898 --> 00:13:56.379
Google is just kind of bumbling its way

00:13:56.379 --> 00:13:58.232
through the process a little bit.

00:13:58.232 --> 00:14:00.111
But over and over again

00:14:00.111 --> 00:14:03.044
Google has put something out in the world,

00:14:03.044 --> 00:14:05.953
like Search, like Street View,

00:14:05.953 --> 00:14:07.620
like Translate, Geo,

00:14:09.745 --> 00:14:13.112
almost everything we've
done has started from a,

00:14:13.112 --> 00:14:14.080
"You know what?

00:14:14.080 --> 00:14:15.549
"This is just the right thing to do.

00:14:15.549 --> 00:14:17.694
"The world is just better this way.

00:14:17.694 --> 00:14:19.894
"Let's start by helping users,

00:14:19.894 --> 00:14:22.068
"and then we'll figure out the money thing

00:14:22.068 --> 00:14:23.134
"in a little while."

00:14:23.134 --> 00:14:24.840
And that just shouldn't be true.

00:14:24.840 --> 00:14:26.562
That's like cheating
in the business world.

00:14:26.562 --> 00:14:28.674
There's something wrong with that.

00:14:28.674 --> 00:14:30.585
But wouldn't that be awesome
if you could do that?

00:14:30.585 --> 00:14:32.712
If you could actually
spend your time saying,

00:14:32.712 --> 00:14:35.639
"Let's just make the world crazy better,"

00:14:35.639 --> 00:14:38.292
and then trust that the money
is going to come find us?

00:14:38.292 --> 00:14:40.787
If you want to know
what you're capable of,

00:14:40.787 --> 00:14:43.787
look backwards, don't look forwards.

00:14:45.303 --> 00:14:47.470
It's so easy to feel like,

00:14:48.401 --> 00:14:52.232
"I don't know how to do it
so I won't get it done."

00:14:52.232 --> 00:14:53.475
But that's kind of nonsensical.

00:14:53.475 --> 00:14:56.285
You've never done this thing before.

00:14:56.285 --> 00:14:59.722
Of course you never know
how to do the next thing.

00:14:59.722 --> 00:15:02.931
It would be boring if you
already knew how to do it.

00:15:02.931 --> 00:15:04.813
But look backwards at all the things

00:15:04.813 --> 00:15:07.733
you personally have
accomplished in your life.

00:15:07.733 --> 00:15:11.471
Imagine going back to the 20
year old version of yourself

00:15:11.471 --> 00:15:14.841
and telling them that they
had to go do all these things.

00:15:14.841 --> 00:15:16.476
It would sound like black magic to them,

00:15:16.476 --> 00:15:18.444
because that person
didn't know how to do it.

00:15:18.444 --> 00:15:20.995
You figured it out as you went.

00:15:20.995 --> 00:15:22.988
I have a CEO that I'm mentoring,

00:15:22.988 --> 00:15:23.821
and he called me,

00:15:23.821 --> 00:15:26.531
and this is like two, three days ago.

00:15:26.531 --> 00:15:29.490
And he said, "Oh God, I have
this board meeting coming up,

00:15:29.490 --> 00:15:30.377
"and I had all these plans

00:15:30.377 --> 00:15:33.024
"about how I was going to ask the board."

00:15:33.024 --> 00:15:33.929
He's got an investor

00:15:33.929 --> 00:15:35.779
who wants to put a couple
million dollars in,

00:15:35.779 --> 00:15:38.384
but the board doesn't want
to take incremental dilution,

00:15:38.384 --> 00:15:39.573
and he was going to like show them

00:15:39.573 --> 00:15:40.998
all these great new results.

00:15:40.998 --> 00:15:43.043
And he just felt like,

00:15:43.043 --> 00:15:44.339
"You know, our revenue
has kind of been flat

00:15:44.339 --> 00:15:45.557
"for the last couple of months.

00:15:45.557 --> 00:15:47.256
"And I had these results and
now I can't show it to them,

00:15:47.256 --> 00:15:50.097
"because they're not as
good as I thought they were.

00:15:50.097 --> 00:15:51.445
"Ah, I'm just I'm so frustrated.

00:15:51.445 --> 00:15:54.779
"I'm just, I'm no good at this."

00:15:54.779 --> 00:15:58.354
I said, "Dave, you're
looking over the last

00:15:58.354 --> 00:16:01.890
"like three weeks, four weeks maybe.

00:16:01.890 --> 00:16:05.796
"You've been the CEO of this
business for 22 months."

00:16:05.796 --> 00:16:07.565
It was already an ongoing business

00:16:07.565 --> 00:16:10.689
when he became the CEO of it.

00:16:10.689 --> 00:16:11.939
"The revenues of this business

00:16:11.939 --> 00:16:15.189
"have gone up a factor 12 in 22 months.

00:16:17.618 --> 00:16:19.424
"You've taken on one person

00:16:19.424 --> 00:16:22.375
"and diluted the company by like 8%

00:16:22.375 --> 00:16:25.445
"and taken up the revenues
by a factor of 12.

00:16:25.445 --> 00:16:27.341
"Seriously, you're a loser?

00:16:27.341 --> 00:16:30.022
"My God, we can only hope as a board

00:16:30.022 --> 00:16:33.683
"that you'll be such a loser
for the next 22 months."

00:16:33.683 --> 00:16:36.489
But it's easy for us to
like laugh and see that,

00:16:36.489 --> 00:16:39.972
but you can see how in the mindset

00:16:39.972 --> 00:16:41.187
you care about something

00:16:41.187 --> 00:16:43.318
and you get so focused on the future

00:16:43.318 --> 00:16:44.532
and what you don't know,

00:16:44.532 --> 00:16:47.791
but if you look backwards
things look very different.

00:16:47.791 --> 00:16:51.944
And often that's what
helps me to take on a task

00:16:51.944 --> 00:16:53.625
that I don't know how to do,

00:16:53.625 --> 00:16:56.571
because I don't have to
prove to myself I believe

00:16:56.571 --> 00:16:58.262
that I know how to do that task.

00:16:58.262 --> 00:17:00.077
I only have to convince myself

00:17:00.077 --> 00:17:01.718
that I've historically done a good job

00:17:01.718 --> 00:17:03.914
of figuring out tasks I
didn't know how to do.

00:17:03.914 --> 00:17:04.747
(swoosh)

00:17:04.747 --> 00:17:05.647
- Thank you so much for watching.

00:17:05.647 --> 00:17:08.881
I made this video because
Blast asked me to.

00:17:08.881 --> 00:17:10.241
So, if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:17:10.241 --> 00:17:12.388
that you want me to profile next,

00:17:12.388 --> 00:17:14.755
leave it in the comments below
and I'll see what I can do.

00:17:14.755 --> 00:17:15.595
I'd also love to know

00:17:15.595 --> 00:17:17.619
which of Astro Teller's top 10 rules

00:17:17.619 --> 00:17:19.083
resonated with you the most.

00:17:19.083 --> 00:17:20.069
Leave it in the comments.

00:17:20.069 --> 00:17:21.361
I will join in the discussion.

00:17:21.361 --> 00:17:22.582
Thank you so much for watching.

00:17:22.582 --> 00:17:25.428
Continue to believe and I'll see you soon.

00:17:25.428 --> 00:17:27.982
(dramatic swoosh)

